## Geometric Preliminaries

**Fact.** Three distinct points on a circle form an obtuse triangle (some angle strictly $> 90°$) if and only if they all lie in some **open semicircle** — equivalently, among the three arcs into which the points divide the circle, one arc is **strictly greater than $180°$**.

*Proof.* An inscribed angle equals half its intercepted arc. An angle exceeds $90°$ iff its intercepted arc exceeds $180°$. The intercepted arc of the angle at one vertex is the arc between the other two vertices that does **not** contain that vertex — i.e., one of the three gaps. So some angle $>90°$ iff some gap $>180°$, iff the three points lie in the complementary arc of length $<180°$ (an open semicircle). $\square$

Consequently, a triple is **non-obtuse** (acute or right) iff every gap is $\le 180°$.

---

## Lemma 1 (Five points force an obtuse triple)

**Any 5 distinct points on a circle contain three that form an obtuse triangle.**

*Proof.* Let the 5 points be $P_1,\dots,P_5$ in circular order, with consecutive gaps $g_1,\dots,g_5>0$ summing to $360°$. Suppose, for contradiction, that every triple is non-obtuse, i.e., every triple has all gaps $\le 180°$.

Consider the five "consecutive" triples $\{P_i,P_{i+1},P_{i+2}\}$ (indices mod 5). For $\{P_1,P_2,P_3\}$, the three gaps are $g_1,\;g_2,\;g_3+g_4+g_5$. Non-obtuseness forces $g_3+g_4+g_5\le 180°$, i.e., $g_1+g_2\ge 180°$. Applying this to each consecutive triple gives

$$g_1+g_2\ge 180°,\quad g_2+g_3\ge 180°,\quad g_3+g_4\ge 180°,\quad g_4+g_5\ge 180°,\quad g_5+g_1\ge 180°.$$

Summing all five inequalities: $2(g_1+\cdots+g_5)\ge 5\cdot 180°=900°$, so $720°\ge 900°$, a contradiction. Hence some triple has a gap $>180°$ and is strictly obtuse. $\square$

---

## Lemma 2 (Four points can avoid obtuse triples)

**There exist 4 distinct points on a circle with no obtuse triple: the vertices of a rectangle** (two antipodal pairs).

*Proof.* Place points at angles $\theta,\;\theta+\alpha,\;\theta+180°,\;\theta+180°+\alpha$ with $0<\alpha<180°$. These form two antipodal pairs. Any triple of three of them contains one antipodal pair, so one of the three gaps equals exactly $180°$ and the other two sum to $180°$ (each $<180°$). Thus every triple is a **right** triangle — non-obtuse. $\square$

Moreover, 4 points avoid all obtuse triples **only** in this rectangle configuration: with gaps $g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4$ (sum $360°$), non-obtuseness of all four triples forces each merged gap $g_i+g_{i+1}\le 180°$; summing gives $720°\le 720°$, so equality holds throughout, yielding $g_1=g_3,\;g_2=g_4,\;g_1+g_2=180°$ — exactly two antipodal pairs.

